name: schnitz / katsu
yield: per 100g meat
variants:
  - name: chicken (the default)
    ingredients:
      - item: chicken
        mass: 100
        basis: true
      - item: salt
        mass: 1.5
      - item: MSG
        mass: 1
      - item: baking soda
        mass: 1
      - item: black pepper
        mass: 0.2
      - item: white pepper
        mass: 0.2
      - item: paprika
        mass: 0.5
      - item: italian herbs
        mass: 0.4
      - item: rosemary leaves
        mass: 0.15
        note: from approx 0.2g in sprig form, with stem; try oregano
      - item: coriander
        mass: 0.15
        note: experiment
      - item: dried ground sage
        mass: 0.1
        note: experiment
      - item: pun chun
        mass: 0.15
        note: experiment, only in egg wash
      - item: panko
        mass: 18
      - item: eggs
        qty: 0.4
        unit: ea
      - item: flour
        mass: 14
    steps:
      - season flour, eggs (beaten), and panko
      - coat chicken breast bits and fry at 160°C (with probe, 200°C if measuring base heat)
  - name: duck
    ingredients:
      - item: duck breast
        mass: 100
        basis: true
        note: weighed skinless and trimmed — ~70% of skin-on weight
      - item: salt
        mass: 1.5
      - item: MSG
        mass: 1
      - item: baking soda
        mass: 0.5
        optional: true
        note: half the chicken amount, or skip — pink duck doesn't need tenderising and too much goes spongy and grey
      - item: black pepper
        mass: 0.2
      - item: white pepper
        mass: 0.2
      - item: paprika
        mass: 0.5
      - item: italian herbs
        mass: 0.4
        note: or swap some for a pinch of 5 spice
      - item: rosemary leaves
        mass: 0.15
      - item: coriander
        mass: 0.15
      - item: dried ground sage
        mass: 0.2
        note: doubled — sage is the duck herb
      - item: pun chun
        mass: 0.15
        note: only in egg wash
      - item: panko
        mass: 18
      - item: eggs
        qty: 0.4
        unit: ea
      - item: flour
        mass: 14
      - item: duck fat
        note: rendered from the skin, for some or all of the frying oil
    steps:
      - take the skin off, trim silverskin, pull off the tenderloin (fry it separately)
      - render the skin for crackling and keep the fat for frying
      - pound gently between cling film with the flat side to an even ~1cm — not chicken-thin, or it cooks through before the crumb browns
      - season just before crumbing, not ahead
      - season flour, eggs (beaten), and panko, then coat
      - chill crumbed cutlets 15-30 mins so the coating sets and the middle starts cold
      - fry starting at 180°C, cooking at 160°C, ~1.5-2 mins a side. pull at 53-54°C internal for 56°C — thin cutlets carry over very little
    notes: |
      Untested as of 2026-10-30. Duck breast is red meat and wants to be pink like
      5-spice-duck; cooked through it goes livery and tough. Everything here is about
      getting the crumb golden before the centre passes 56°C: thicker cutlet, hotter start,
      cold going in.

      Wild duck is much leaner and tougher — pound it thinner and accept it'll cook further.

      Serve with something sharp and fruity: lingonberry, cherry, plum, orange, or lemon.
